WEBVTT

00:00:01.208 --> 00:00:06.667
Alexandria’s lighthouse appears on a
bronze ring, according to its excavators.

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:09.333
They found it at a submerged palace.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.625
But does a lighthouse image identify
a lighthouse official?

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:17.417
That is the connection proposed by
the excavation team.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:22.875
If it holds, a portable object could
help explain a palace’s later use.

00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:26.625
The picture alone cannot settle that
connection.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:31.500
On October seventh, twenty twenty-six, the
team announced the find from Antirhodos.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:36.000
This submerged island belonged to
Alexandria’s ancient royal quarter.

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:39.625
The announcement calls the object a
bronze ring-signet.

00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:41.292
The team describes it as the

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:45.500
first known bronze ring-signet
depicting the lighthouse.

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:49.542
That does not mean the first ancient
lighthouse image.

00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:53.208
A museum coin gives us an
independent comparison.

00:00:52.708 --> 00:00:57.708
What can one ring tell us about
Alexandria’s submerged royal quarter?

00:00:57.875 --> 00:01:02.083
We can compare the picture, examine
the proposed function,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.917
and separate the object’s date from
the age of its surroundings.

00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:10.875
One question concerns what the
palace setting establishes.

00:01:11.042 --> 00:01:15.375
And can the nearby pottery tell us
when the ring was made?

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:18.583
They require different kinds of
evidence.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.750
First, look at the object type.

00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:27.458
The team identifies a signet, not simply
an ornament with a recognizable picture.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:31.125
What would such a seal allow someone
to do?

00:01:30.792 --> 00:01:36.083
A signet can press an identifying
design into a soft material.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.583
Think of a stamp carried with its
user.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.125
The mark can travel beyond the
object that made it.

00:01:43.792 --> 00:01:48.917
That explains a possible practical
role. It does not show which documents,

00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:53.000
containers, or transactions this
particular ring touched.

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:57.125
For that, a general explanation of
signets is not enough.

00:01:56.792 --> 00:02:00.917
The Metropolitan Museum describes
ancient engraved gems used as seals.

00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:05.125
Their impressions could establish
ownership, authenticate documents,

00:02:05.292 --> 00:02:10.083
or protect private letters. The museum
also records ornamental and amuletic uses.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.333
Those examples concern engraved
gems.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.917
Our subject is the bronze ring
described by the excavation team.

00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:21.167
The comparison explains possible
functions without claiming

00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:23.708
that all signets had the same job.

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:27.292
A second museum object makes the
distinction concrete.

00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:30.833
The Met holds a clay bulla from
ancient Iran.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.625
Four impressions repeat an oval seal
design featuring a moth.

00:02:35.292 --> 00:02:40.500
The museum says a signet ring may
have produced those impressions.

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:44.125
It also says the clay may have
sealed a jar.

00:02:44.292 --> 00:02:47.750
Neither identification is stated as
certain.

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:51.333
This is comparative evidence, not an
Alexandria find.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.375
It shows how a small image could
belong to an accounting practice.

00:02:56.542 --> 00:03:01.625
It does not transfer that particular
practice to the lighthouse ring.

00:03:01.292 --> 00:03:05.750
Now compare the lighthouse image
with something securely cataloged.

00:03:05.917 --> 00:03:10.333
The British Museum holds a
copper-alloy coin minted in Alexandria,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.708
dated to AD one thirty-three to one
thirty-four.

00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:16.708
Its catalog describes Hadrian’s head
on one side.

00:03:16.875 --> 00:03:21.625
The other includes Isis Pharia and
the Pharos, Alexandria’s lighthouse.

00:03:21.792 --> 00:03:26.292
Here is an earlier lighthouse
representation on a different object.

00:03:25.958 --> 00:03:32.583
That answers the novelty question. The
ring is reported as a new kind of example,

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.250
not the first surviving image of the
tower.

00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:40.583
Its setting and possible use are the
new questions.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:44.208
But what does recognizing a landmark
actually identify?

00:03:44.375 --> 00:03:47.333
It can identify the subject of a
picture.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:53.583
It cannot, without more evidence, identify
the occupation of the person carrying it.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.375
Imagine a modern city emblem.

00:03:55.542 --> 00:03:59.708
Recognizing the building tells you
which place it evokes,

00:03:59.875 --> 00:04:01.875
not its owner’s profession.

00:04:02.042 --> 00:04:07.167
This is an analogy, not evidence
about the ancient ring’s actual user.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:09.250
The picture has taken us only so
far.

00:04:09.417 --> 00:04:14.792
The administrative interpretation also
depends on where the object was found.

00:04:14.958 --> 00:04:20.000
How does an underwater object acquire
a recorded archaeological setting?

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:22.792
The project’s published methods
begin before excavation.

00:04:22.958 --> 00:04:25.875
Archives and ancient texts help
guide investigation.

00:04:26.042 --> 00:04:28.417
Non-intrusive instruments then map
features

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:31.833
that researchers may investigate
through test excavations.

00:04:31.333 --> 00:04:37.083
One technique is multibeam bathymetry. It
uses sound to measure underwater relief.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.292
In plain language, it maps the
bottom’s hills and hollows,

00:04:41.458 --> 00:04:45.083
rather than identifying the
occupants of a building.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:48.625
The methods account also describes
side-scan sonar,

00:04:48.792 --> 00:04:52.708
which records acoustic contrasts and
object shadows.

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:58.958
A feature on a survey image is a lead to
investigate, not a named room or owner.

00:04:58.625 --> 00:05:02.917
Excavation adds inventories, drawings,
photographs, and mapped records.

00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:06.583
Archaeological context means those
recorded relationships.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:11.500
Put simply, it is the object together
with the things and structures around it.

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:15.167
That gives the palace setting a role
in the interpretation.

00:05:15.333 --> 00:05:20.083
Goddio connects the ring with
lighthouse-related administration there.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.500
The claim concerns a possible use of
the former royal building.

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:27.292
An Alexandrian merchant is another
possibility.

00:05:27.458 --> 00:05:30.750
Scholar Margaret Miles suggested
that alternative,

00:05:30.917 --> 00:05:34.292
as reported by Live Science and
relayed by UA.News.

00:05:34.458 --> 00:05:38.083
The lighthouse could signal a
connection with the city.

00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:39.042
This changes the test.

00:05:39.208 --> 00:05:43.708
Recognizing the tower is no longer
enough to identify an occupation.

00:05:43.875 --> 00:05:47.083
We have a reported administrative
interpretation

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.500
and a reported merchant alternative,
not two established owners.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.833
The merchant suggestion does not prove
the administrative proposal wrong.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.125
Nor does finding the ring at a palace
automatically prove that proposal right.

00:06:01.292 --> 00:06:05.208
The same recognizable image can
support different readings.

00:06:04.875 --> 00:06:08.500
A hypothesis is an explanation that
needs testing.

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:13.208
In everyday language, it is a
possible answer, not the result.

00:06:13.375 --> 00:06:18.625
Here, the published material reviewed
does not identify a specific user.

00:06:18.292 --> 00:06:22.625
What would be lost if the
surrounding site went unrecorded?

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:27.500
The object alone cannot answer every
question about its setting.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:32.083
To test the proposed office, start
with the dating evidence.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.500
The announcement reports the ring
closely associated

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:39.000
with intact ceramics of the second
century AD.

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:42.667
It also explicitly allows the ring
to be earlier.

00:06:42.833 --> 00:06:45.667
Those statements must be read
together.

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:50.667
The pottery association and the ring’s
manufacture are different questions.

00:06:50.833 --> 00:06:55.042
The team uses the context to
interpret its period of use.

00:06:55.208 --> 00:06:58.500
It does not give an exact
manufacturing year.

00:06:58.167 --> 00:07:03.167
Consider a hypothetical example: an
old keepsake beside a dated receipt.

00:07:03.333 --> 00:07:08.625
The receipt does not make the keepsake
new. This illustrates the distinction;

00:07:08.792 --> 00:07:12.333
it is not the excavation’s actual
dating procedure.

00:07:12.042 --> 00:07:14.167
That resolves our pottery question.

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:17.333
The association informs the team’s
interpretation,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.833
while an earlier ring remains
possible.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.583
Assigning the pottery’s century
directly to

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:26.125
manufacture would remove the
report’s own qualification.

00:07:25.458 --> 00:07:28.625
The palace’s royal history also
needs care.

00:07:28.792 --> 00:07:32.125
A former royal building can have a
later use.

00:07:32.292 --> 00:07:37.292
The team proposes administrative
activity in a Roman-period context,

00:07:37.458 --> 00:07:40.250
rather than naming a royal ring
owner.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:44.167
A useful physical detail survives in
the announcement.

00:07:44.333 --> 00:07:48.750
Opposite the setting is a small tab
with a round hole.

00:07:48.917 --> 00:07:54.375
The team says a fine cord could
suspend the object around the neck.

00:07:54.042 --> 00:07:56.708
That suggests one possible way to
carry it.

00:07:56.875 --> 00:08:01.417
A suspension hole does not identify
an occupation either. Object features,

00:08:01.583 --> 00:08:03.250
archaeological association,

00:08:03.417 --> 00:08:08.000
and historical interpretation answer
different parts of the investigation.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.125
Separate the claims: the team
reports the object and its context.

00:08:12.292 --> 00:08:16.083
It proposes a later administrative
use of the palace.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:22.125
The available account does not turn that
proposal into an identified officeholder.

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:27.375
One question remains about the method
itself. Why record the surrounding site

00:08:27.542 --> 00:08:31.500
rather than treating the object as
the whole discovery?

00:08:31.667 --> 00:08:36.000
The answer also explains what would
be lost without context.

00:08:35.667 --> 00:08:39.292
UNESCO’s account of its underwater
heritage convention

00:08:39.458 --> 00:08:42.750
treats preservation in place as the
first option.

00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:48.250
It also allows authorized recovery that
contributes significantly to protection

00:08:48.417 --> 00:08:51.792
or knowledge. It is not a blanket
ban on recovery.

00:08:51.292 --> 00:08:57.083
The point is to preserve and understand
relationships, not merely collect objects.

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:01.667
In situ means in place. An inventory
and a mapped setting keep

00:09:01.833 --> 00:09:05.500
more information than an isolated
object photograph.

00:09:05.167 --> 00:09:07.042
Return to the palace question.

00:09:07.208 --> 00:09:12.000
The ring’s reported setting contributes
to the administrative interpretation.

00:09:12.167 --> 00:09:13.583
But a reported findspot

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:18.625
and a recognizable lighthouse do not
uniquely establish its user’s occupation.

00:09:18.125 --> 00:09:21.750
The merchant suggestion demonstrates
that limit.

00:09:21.917 --> 00:09:26.083
We have not shown the two proposals
are equally likely.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:31.000
We have shown why the image alone
does not choose between them.

00:09:30.708 --> 00:09:34.333
Three pieces remain distinct: the
reported palace find,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.750
the museum evidence for older
lighthouse imagery,

00:09:37.917 --> 00:09:40.292
and the general practice of sealing.

00:09:40.458 --> 00:09:45.042
Bringing them together gives context
without producing a named owner.

00:09:44.542 --> 00:09:48.333
The strongest responsible answer is
therefore a bounded one.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:50.708
The ring adds a reported lighthouse

00:09:50.875 --> 00:09:54.000
image to the palace site’s
archaeological record.

00:09:54.167 --> 00:09:58.917
Its proposed administrative significance
remains an interpretation to test.

00:09:58.417 --> 00:10:04.167
In plain words, the object helps frame a
question about the building’s later use.

00:10:04.333 --> 00:10:06.583
It does not finish that history.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.375
Better contextual evidence could
sharpen the answer

00:10:10.542 --> 00:10:13.667
without changing what was actually
reported.

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:15.667
A related investigation could follow

00:10:15.833 --> 00:10:21.667
how survey features become archaeological
sites. Here, the larger lesson is smaller:

00:10:21.833 --> 00:10:24.458
recognizing a monument is not the
same

00:10:24.625 --> 00:10:27.958
as identifying the person who
carried its image.

00:10:27.458 --> 00:10:31.167
That person’s identity is the honest
open thread:

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:37.458
the sources reviewed offer possibilities,
not a name. We can recognize the tower.

00:10:37.625 --> 00:10:39.917
We still cannot name the hand.
